# User Magnus Melin <mkmelin+mozilla@iki.fi>
# Date 1793905985 -7200
# Node ID ae6bd7d8dc1053184ef63bbc9eedaca3993b0675
# Parent  13cad0e49808cf7ff7cb81c9324a0cd681b0b791
Bug 2069949 - Don't throw when an outgoing server has no hostname. r=edicharry

nsIMsgOutgoingServerService.createServer() appends the new key to
mail.smtpservers before its caller has set a hostname, so an interrupted
account setup can leave an SMTP server behind that has no hostname but does
have a username or a port. SmtpServer builds its URI by handing
"smtp://" + [user@] + hostname + [:port] to Services.io.newURI(), which
rejects a spec with an empty host next to an authority, such as
"smtp://user@" or "smtp://:587". A bare "smtp://" does parse, which is why
only some profiles are affected.

Bug 1882574 changed nsIMsgOutgoingServer.serverURI from a string into that
parsed nsIURI and moved its consumers off the hostname and port prefs onto
nsIURI.host and nsIURI.port, so every one of them now throws
NS_ERROR_MALFORMED_URI on such a server:

    checkOutgoingServerAlreadyExists() walks the whole outgoing server list, so account creation fails regardless of what the user enters or whether they are online (Bug 2069949).
    loadSMTPServerList() throws out of the identity pane's onPreInit, leaving the identity fields blank and unable to keep changes (Bug 2069799).
    The Outgoing Server pane, its deletion prompt and SmtpServerEdit fail too, so the offending server cannot even be inspected or removed.

Fix this in the accessor rather than at each consumer: serverURI returns null
for a server with no hostname, and for a hostname that yields no parseable
URI, and nsIMsgOutgoingServer documents it as nullable. The consumers then
read it plainly and fall back to smtpServerList-NotSpecified for display, so
an unconfigured server stays listed, editable and deletable. findServer()
already used optional chaining and needs no change.

Removing such a server is not enough on its own: an identity whose
smtpServerKey named it would resolve to no server at all rather than to the
default, which makes MessageSend refuse to send. getServerByIdentity() now
treats an unresolvable key like an absent one, which also covers the profiles
whose users already removed the entry by hand from prefs.js, and the migration
clears smtpServerKey on the identities that referenced what it removes, the
way replaceWithDefaultSmtpServer() does for the Outgoing Server pane.

Where it is cheap to do so, stop producing the state as well: SmtpServerEdit's
accept handler removes the server it just created when saving fails, instead
of only logging and still reporting success, and the Sync servers engine
checks a record's location before creating anything from it. A UI version
migration drops SMTP servers that have no hostname, so profiles that already
hit this recover without hand-editing prefs.

The EWS and Graph outgoing servers still throw from serverURI and username
when their own prefs are missing, which is the same defect one protocol over;
that is bug 2067724.

Differential Revision: https://phabricator.services.mozilla.com/D325420

